{"metadata":{"role":"article","roleHeading":"Article","title":"Accessibility","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}]},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A placeholder is often the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"only"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" thing on screen for a few hundred milliseconds, or the only thing a user with a slow connection sees for much longer than that — so it’s worth it being as accessible as the real content it stands in for. The default ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStateDefault{Empty,Loading,Error}View","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"UIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"s handle all of this out of the box; this article rounds up what they do and how a fully custom placeholder can match them.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"VoiceOver-announcements","text":"VoiceOver announcements"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each default placeholder announces its own appearance — ","type":"text"},{"code":"AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(_:).post()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" in SwiftUI, ","type":"text"},{"code":"UIAccessibility.post(notification:.announcement, argument:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" in UIKit — so a screen settling into ","type":"text"},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":".loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", or ","type":"text"},{"code":".error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is heard, not just seen.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement"},{"type":"text","text":" exposes that same wording for any case, so a fully custom placeholder can announce transitions consistently instead of inventing its own text:"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":[".onAppear {","    if let announcement = state.accessibilityAnnouncement {","        AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(announcement).post()","    }","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"It’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":" for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".data"},{"type":"text","text":" — your own content is what VoiceOver should read at that point — and, for "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".empty"},{"type":"text","text":", it’s the default “Nothing Here” text; announce your own custom title directly instead if your placeholder shows one, so VoiceOver matches what’s on screen. See "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" for the full examples."}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Reduce Motion","anchor":"Reduce-Motion"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The default Loading placeholder’s ring spins via a repeating rotation animation — purely decorative, and exactly the kind of large, continuous motion Reduce Motion exists to suppress. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"text":" reads SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"accessibilityReduceMotion"},{"text":" environment value; ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView"},{"text":" reads ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.isReduceMotionEnabled"},{"text":" and also observes ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.reduceMotionStatusDidChangeNotification"},{"text":", so an already-visible spinner responds immediately if the setting changes while on screen, not just on the next appearance. Either way, the ring stays static — still colorful, just not spinning — instead of disabling itself entirely, since a static ring still communicates “loading” on its own.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Increase Contrast","anchor":"Increase-Contrast"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"All three default placeholders’ gradient icons\/ring respect Increase Contrast — SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"colorSchemeContrast"},{"text":" environment value, UIKit’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.isDarkerSystemColorsEnabled"},{"text":" (observed live the same way Reduce Motion is). A multi-stop gradient can dip below an acceptable contrast ratio in places a single flat color never does, so when Increase Contrast is on, every gradient is swapped for a solid ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".label"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".primary"},{"text":" tint instead.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Dynamic Type","anchor":"Dynamic-Type"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The default Empty\/Error placeholders’ text scales with Dynamic Type: SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"ErrorView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" get this for free from ","type":"text"},{"code":"ContentUnavailableView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s own text styles, and ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"ErrorUIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s labels use ","type":"text"},{"code":"UIFont.preferredFont(forTextStyle: .body, compatibleWith:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Both are covered up to the largest accessibility size by snapshot tests, so a regression that silently pins the text to a fixed size would be caught. The default Loading placeholder has no text at all — its ring is a fixed-size shape, unaffected by Dynamic Type by design.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Identifiers for XCUITest","anchor":"Identifiers-for-XCUITest"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every default placeholder exposes a stable "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"accessibilityIdentifier"},{"type":"text","text":", independent of any localized or custom text, so a UI test can assert which state is showing without string-matching:"}]},{"type":"table","rows":[[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Identifier"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Shown on","type":"text"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.loading","type":"codeVoice"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.empty","type":"codeVoice"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"}]}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.error"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","isActive":true},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView","isAc